# SSR First — Server Rendering Is The Default

Akan is SSR-first. A JSX element rendered on the server ships as HTML and costs nothing to hydrate; an element
behind `"use client"` ships twice — as markup and as bundled JS that re-runs in the browser. The boundary question
is not which file *may* be client, it is **how little** ends up on the client side.

- **The default is server. `"use client"` is a cost you justify per component, not a habit.** A component earns the
  directive only by using a client-only capability: a React hook, a JSX event handler, the store (`st.use.*` /
  `st.do.*`), a browser global, or a client-only third-party package. Rendering markup, reading a param, calling
  `l()`, and mapping over data are server work.
- Measure with `akan quality ssr` (`--format json` for tooling). It prints the server render share per app and lib.
  Treat **50% server share as the floor** and a falling share as a regression.

## What `akan quality ssr` Flags

- `akan.ssr.unnecessary-use-client` — the directive is there but nothing in the file needs it. Delete it.
- `akan.ssr.client-static-component` — a component in a client file renders real markup with zero client-only
  capability: pure server work sitting in the bundle.
- `akan.ssr.client-static-markup` — a large subtree wraps one or two interactive touches. Split it.
- `akan.ssr.client-mount-load` — a `useEffect(…, [])` loads server data the route could fetch first.
- `akan.ssr.module-missing-server-view` — a module renders only from `Template`/`Zone`/`Util`, with no `Unit`/`View`.
- `akan.ssr.template-client-state` — a `Template` holds form state in `useState` instead of the store.

A third-party client package or an `index_.tsx` `lazy()` boundary justifies the directive and is not flagged.
Interaction-driven `fetch.*` inside `onClick` is not flagged either — only mount-time loads are.

## Server-Side Implementation Playbook

1. **Wrap the interaction, not the UI.** The smallest useful client component adds one behaviour and renders
   `children` untouched, so the markup inside never reaches the bundle (`libs/shared/ui/Only/User.tsx`).
2. **Split compound components so panels stay on the server.** A tab or accordion needs client state only for
   *which* part is visible. `Tab` / `Tab.Menus` / `Tab.Menu` / `Tab.Panel` in `akanjs/ui` is the shape: only the
   provider and menu hold state, and `<Tab.Panel>` renders children as-is. Never one client file with a mode
   `useState` and every panel body inlined.
3. **Sync state instead of fetching it.** The route calls `fetch.initXInY(...)` / `fetch.viewX(...)` and passes the
   serializable result into a `Zone`; `Load.Units` / `Load.View` hydrate the store from it. Never a
   `useEffect(…, [])` that fetches on mount.
4. **Push the boundary down to the leaf that needs it.** A store-reading `Zone` holds zero markup and delegates to
   a server `View` (`User.Zone.Self` → `<User.View.General user={self} />`).
5. **Hand the promise across, not the awaited value.** `ClientInit` / `ClientView` are `PromiseOrObject<T>`, so a
   route may pass an unawaited `fetch.initX(...)` and `Load.*` resolves it behind a skeleton.
6. **Use named `ReactNode` slots, not just `children`.** `Layout.Navbar` takes `title`, `back`, `left`, `right`,
   and `children`, so a client shell composes server content in five places.
7. **Let the server do the derived work.** Display and predicate logic lives on `Light<Model>` (`isNew()`,
   `canWrite(user?)`, `formatTimes()`); enum→class lookups live in a module-scope `as const` map.
8. **Gate auth on the server.** `getSelf({ unauthorize: "/signin" })` in `_layout.tsx` redirects before any HTML is
   sent; a client-side check costs a round-trip and flashes the wrong UI.
9. **Prefer CSS over client state for pure visibility.** A `data-*` attribute plus `group-data-[…]` variants (see
   `libs/util/ui/Grid/*`) or `<details>`/`<summary>` keeps both branches server-rendered.
10. **Keep the heavy island out of the first load.** Put a large client-only widget behind the
    `ui/<Folder>/index_.tsx` + `lazy()` pair. `usePage()` and `l()` work in server components, so translation never
    forces a boundary.
